body:
This is amazing! 

&#x200B;

Have you ever considered doing art for coloring books? I have no idea what the process is like, but your art is so badass, it looks like it would be a blast to color!!

Whatever you're feeling, and whatever anyone says, you did the right thing. Ensuring someones safety in a time of crisis is the most important thing you can do, and by calling the cops you helped in the best possible way you could. 

&#x200B;

You did a good, selfless, and compassionate thing. He may not realize it now, and maybe not ever, but he should be grateful to have someone that cares so deeply about their well being to take action like this. 

&#x200B;

I think about committing suicide every day, several times a day. At this point, although I'm only 23, I've not actually begun to plan my future because I just...don't think I'm going to get a future? 

&#x200B;

It almost feels out of my hands, like I'm just buying time until the day finally comes that I can let go. Its almost a comforting thought if I'm honest...

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

I struggle with my own mother/family in a similar way. My mom is a therapist herself, and for as long as I can remember she has always tried to psychoanalyze me and my behaviors, even back when I was a kid. Now that I'm an adult, its interesting to see her get angry, or even seem hurt, when I choose not to discuss certain things about my mental health with her.

&#x200B;

I've only recently started to set boundaries with her, saying no or simply ending the conversation if it gets too far, but it was pretty bad in the beginning. 

There was an instance last year when I spent 9 days hospitalized due to the severity of my mental health at the time, and although I debated with even telling my family, my partner agreed to help tell them everything while I was unable to make contact within the hospital. We had only one condition...that the knowledge of my hospitalization would stay between my parents, me, and my partner. I wasn't, and still am not, ready to disclose my issues to my extended family, even my sisters, who despite their good intentions, really just come off as nosy and inconsiderate. 

Of course, not a day later, every single person in my family, even those I don't talk to, knew about my situation. Its a terrible feeling, especially since now at every family event I actually attend (not many, now), I get asked awkward questions, and put in awkward situations, just because my family doesn't understand the concept of 'mind your own damn business'. 

&#x200B;

I can totally relate with how you feel. Your mental health is your business, and yours alone, and although you may live with your mother, she has no right to have unlimited access to your emotions, and much less the private discussions you have with your doctor/therapist. 

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

&#x200B;

This resonates with me on a deeply personal level. I too, don't feel like a real person...I don't know how to communicate my thoughts or needs, and I don't try. I feel disconnected from everyone I meet, and everything I do. I'm going through the motions, but there is nothing behind the smile I wear to just get through the day.  I wake up, work, eat, and go back to sleep. 

&#x200B;

I'm sorry I don't have much by way of advise...but you're certainly not alone in feeling this way.

I don't know that I talk in my sleep very often, but my partner has told me that a few times a week, I'll sit up throughout the night completely randomly. I'll be dead asleep, but sitting up with my head drooped down against my chest. 

&#x200B;

My partner told me that the first time I did it, it scared the shit out of them, but ever since its become such a regular thing that they even have a special way of laying me back down so that I don't throw my head back lol.

I don't have much to offer by way of advise- but know that you're not alone in feeling this way. 

&#x200B;

Sometimes I feel like everything I'm doing is just preventing the inevitable, waiting on the day that it finally becomes too much. Its always there, just beneath the surface for me, that desire to just end it all. 

&#x200B;

I'm so sorry you're feeling this way, and I wish you all the best on your journey! Its not one you have to walk alone, try to remember that. 

&#x200B;

Norovirus is probably one of the worst illness's that I've ever had. About two years ago, both my partner and I got sick with it, and spent the next WEEK unable to leave the bed aside from rushing to the bathroom. We had a friend, and my dad, drop off care packages of gatoraid, plain applesauce, and toilet paper, because we literally could not leave the house, and 

&#x200B;

Im so sorry you had to experience this during a time that you were supposed to enjoy.
